<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#litunga">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Litunga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Litunga, meaning 'keeper of the earth', is the title of the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi people</a> of Barotseland in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. The Litunga ruled through a council (kuta) of titled officials and controlled land, labour and tribute across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> floodplain. The office survives today as a traditional monarchy with its seats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lealui">Lealui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limulunga">Limulunga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/litunga.jpg" alt="Litunga" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jae zambia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Litunga_in_2010s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Litunga lives near the Zambezi River and the town of Mongu: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lealui">Lealui</a> on the floodplain in the dry season, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limulunga">Limulunga</a>, on higher ground at the edge of the floodplain, in the wet season. The move between the two seats is made in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomboka">Kuomboka</a> ceremony. The south of the kingdom is ruled by a woman, the Litunga la Mboela or Queen of the South, who is subordinate to the Litunga in the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The line of holders of the title Mbumu wa Litunga begins with Nyambe, a god, and Mwanasolundwi Muyunda Mumbo wa Mulonga, a demigod, followed by rulers from Inyambo and Yeta I to Mwanawina I and Mwananyanda Liwale. Mulambwa Santulu reigned from 1780 to 1830. He was followed by his sons Silumelume and Mubukwanu, who ruled until 1838, and by Mubukwanu's son Imasiku in that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1838 the Makololo held power, their chiefs bearing the title Morêna: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebetwane">Sebetwane</a> until 1851, his daughter Mamochisane, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sekeletu">Sekeletu</a> from 1851 to 1863. After the brief rule of Mambili and the rebellion of Liswaniso in 1863, Sebetwane's brother Mbololo ruled until 1864. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipopa">Sipopa Lutangu</a> then restored the title of Litunga and ruled until 1876. Lubosi I, later known as Lewanika I, reigned from 1878 to 1884 and, after the short rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akufuna-tatila">Akufuna Tatila</a>, again from 1885 to 1916.</p><p>Sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewanika">Lewanika</a> held the title for most of the 20th century: Litia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeta-iii">Yeta III</a> from 1916 to 1945, Imwiko Lewanika from 1946 to 1948, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanawina-iii">Mwanawina III</a> from 1948 to 1968 and Godwin Mbikusita-Lewanika, Lewanika II, from 1968 to 1977, with regents in the intervals. Ilute Yeta IV, son of Yeta III, followed until 2000, when Lubosi Imwiko II, son of Imwiko Lewanika, became Litunga.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Litunga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oral tradition names a woman, Mbuywamwambwa, as the ancestress of the royal line and her son Mboo as the first <b>Litunga</b>, or king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
